[{:db/id "da268a2e-44cb-5d08-8af4-2fcd394f38bd" :answer/id #uuid "da268a2e-44cb-5d08-8af4-2fcd394f38bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "32f421d3-0ff0-50e3-990e-8e56151c2e33" :answer/id #uuid "32f421d3-0ff0-50e3-990e-8e56151c2e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "56eb8950-e15e-52f0-ae16-fb7586994618" :answer/id #uuid "56eb8950-e15e-52f0-ae16-fb7586994618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "8e134253-f04d-5625-a0ee-1441bd7c69d6" :answer/id #uuid "8e134253-f04d-5625-a0ee-1441bd7c69d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "cc9b8357-5dc4-52c7-bb8c-ef2afae597fb" :answer/id #uuid "cc9b8357-5dc4-52c7-bb8c-ef2afae597fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "639fe49f-92ca-5726-bea7-608bd05da2a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "639fe49f-92ca-5726-bea7-608bd05da2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["da268a2e-44cb-5d08-8af4-2fcd394f38bd" "32f421d3-0ff0-50e3-990e-8e56151c2e33" "56eb8950-e15e-52f0-ae16-fb7586994618" "8e134253-f04d-5625-a0ee-1441bd7c69d6" "cc9b8357-5dc4-52c7-bb8c-ef2afae597fb"] :answer-field/correct "da268a2e-44cb-5d08-8af4-2fcd394f38bd" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" :question/id #uuid "5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- 3 y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- 3 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["639fe49f-92ca-5726-bea7-608bd05da2a6"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "0ece7d5e-e40b-5322-bfbd-10fda82d3db3" :answer/id #uuid "0ece7d5e-e40b-5322-bfbd-10fda82d3db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4852ea1f-8754-5a01-89c0-e3f5916b4530" :answer/id #uuid "4852ea1f-8754-5a01-89c0-e3f5916b4530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "5a844f1c-aba0-5582-9d00-7703dff22261" :answer/id #uuid "5a844f1c-aba0-5582-9d00-7703dff22261" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d3f71ec9-9847-53ee-b0ad-cc4c31b80a6d" :answer/id #uuid "d3f71ec9-9847-53ee-b0ad-cc4c31b80a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f51b6044-fe39-551e-9675-ce6e2d45770d" :answer/id #uuid "f51b6044-fe39-551e-9675-ce6e2d45770d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c58ed758-635e-5ca0-8b27-580bc7221389" :answer-field/id #uuid "c58ed758-635e-5ca0-8b27-580bc7221389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["0ece7d5e-e40b-5322-bfbd-10fda82d3db3" "4852ea1f-8754-5a01-89c0-e3f5916b4530" "5a844f1c-aba0-5582-9d00-7703dff22261" "d3f71ec9-9847-53ee-b0ad-cc4c31b80a6d" "f51b6044-fe39-551e-9675-ce6e2d45770d"] :answer-field/correct "0ece7d5e-e40b-5322-bfbd-10fda82d3db3" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "c2766a0b-7719-5fca-8223-c9d01bd4c6c3" :question/id #uuid "c2766a0b-7719-5fca-8223-c9d01bd4c6c3" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{3 y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle -2,-2\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle -1,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)-f(-2,-2) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}-6-6 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["c58ed758-635e-5ca0-8b27-580bc7221389"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "f15f424a-8343-5761-8e46-b77b237d01af" :answer/id #uuid "f15f424a-8343-5761-8e46-b77b237d01af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "2b16d35f-0ee6-51e5-baa1-4b69ca4200ee" :answer/id #uuid "2b16d35f-0ee6-51e5-baa1-4b69ca4200ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0dea8cf4-abca-5534-a7f2-29d2bbc6488c" :answer/id #uuid "0dea8cf4-abca-5534-a7f2-29d2bbc6488c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "998816b6-609b-5a13-94bc-6d1fc0091e92" :answer/id #uuid "998816b6-609b-5a13-94bc-6d1fc0091e92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "542a3357-432b-562c-913e-663a74e70ad4" :answer/id #uuid "542a3357-432b-562c-913e-663a74e70ad4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "206b27c4-d78b-5d3d-a971-63758f948d29" :answer-field/id #uuid "206b27c4-d78b-5d3d-a971-63758f948d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["f15f424a-8343-5761-8e46-b77b237d01af" "2b16d35f-0ee6-51e5-baa1-4b69ca4200ee" "0dea8cf4-abca-5534-a7f2-29d2bbc6488c" "998816b6-609b-5a13-94bc-6d1fc0091e92" "542a3357-432b-562c-913e-663a74e70ad4"] :answer-field/correct "f15f424a-8343-5761-8e46-b77b237d01af" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "2c0d33b2-47e5-55ae-b760-568401b27bca" :question/id #uuid "2c0d33b2-47e5-55ae-b760-568401b27bca" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["206b27c4-d78b-5d3d-a971-63758f948d29"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "97a9befc