ables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["f9adcd3d-8721-5ea1-92cd-e467acbfd7cf"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "fb022fd9-b223-5e5f-8dd8-df9398c24466" :answer/id #uuid "fb022fd9-b223-5e5f-8dd8-df9398c24466" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "64249498-a798-535b-9188-cb51e727cda9" :answer/id #uuid "64249498-a798-535b-9188-cb51e727cda9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "187aaf9a-23e2-5249-9777-13f85fb382b8" :answer/id #uuid "187aaf9a-23e2-5249-9777-13f85fb382b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "bfbf1197-1026-5682-934d-702aeaecd9ee" :answer/id #uuid "bfbf1197-1026-5682-934d-702aeaecd9ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1a0d9f94-0a0a-5915-9dee-44c0efe84a27" :answer/id #uuid "1a0d9f94-0a0a-5915-9dee-44c0efe84a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "b5939e5b-1a1e-5cfa-9cdb-592cf05e8314" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5939e5b-1a1e-5cfa-9cdb-592cf05e8314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["fb022fd9-b223-5e5f-8dd8-df9398c24466" "64249498-a798-535b-9188-cb51e727cda9" "187aaf9a-23e2-5249-9777-13f85fb382b8" "bfbf1197-1026-5682-934d-702aeaecd9ee" "1a0d9f94-0a0a-5915-9dee-44c0efe84a27"] :answer-field/correct "fb022fd9-b223-5e5f-8dd8-df9398c24466" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" :question/id #uuid "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+4- \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["b5939e5b-1a1e-5cfa-9cdb-592cf05e8314"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "d7fc0238-69de-524e-b760-febf67f8aa31" :answer/id #uuid "d7fc0238-69de-524e-b760-febf67f8aa31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a8b7102d-f815-5dd3-8623-99aa71c67037" :answer/id #uuid "a8b7102d-f815-5dd3-8623-99aa71c67037" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2 + 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3cfec755-6997-5f15-9fed-98e9c1c3f129" :answer/id #uuid "3cfec755-6997-5f15-9fed-98e9c1c3f129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 - 4 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "373cde8b-2ba1-502e-9e79-9a586dd3b83b" :answer/id #uuid "373cde8b-2ba1-502e-9e79-9a586dd3b83b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1 - e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "dc3fe867-4ad2-521d-9672-37b784918df2" :answer/id #uuid "dc3fe867-4ad2-521d-9672-37b784918df2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0168e73e-a498-5adc-add3-09c8a7057194" :answer-field/id #uuid "0168e73e-a498-5adc-add3-09c8a7057194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["d7fc0238-69de-524e-b760-febf67f8aa31" "a8b7102d-f815-5dd3-8623-99aa71c67037" "3cfec755-6997-5f15-9fed-98e9c1c3f129" "373cde8b-2ba1-502e-9e79-9a586dd3b83b" "dc3fe867-4ad2-521d-9672-37b784918df2"] :answer-field/correct "d7fc0238-69de-524e-b760-febf67f8aa31" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7" :question/id #uuid "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["0168e73e-a498-5adc-add3-09c8a7057194"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "2a6bb8ac-5c52-5872-bb16-3a7edda9efd7" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133879.py"} [:db/add 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/questions ["5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" "c2766a0b-7719-5fca-8223-c9d01bd4c6c3" "2c0d33b2-47e5-55ae-b760-568401b27bca" "c9033e6e-7d03-5cf3-9e4f-e08eff1e5a07" "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7"]} [:db/ensure 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/validate]]
